# Line endings, decided once for both machines.
#
# This project is written on a Mac and on Windows. Without this file the two
# check out different bytes for the same commit, and `lint-staged` runs
# `prettier --write` — whose config sets `endOfLine: lf` — on every staged file.
# The result is that each developer silently rewrites the other's line endings
# for whatever files they happen to touch, so every diff carries a tail of
# unrelated whole-file changes and the repo converts one file at a time forever.
#
# `eol=lf` normalises in the repository *and* in the working copy, which is what
# makes `prettier --check` pass on Windows. It overrides `core.autocrlf`, so
# neither developer has to configure anything locally.
#
# Editors handle LF on Windows without complaint — VS Code, WebStorm and modern
# Notepad all do. The one thing that does not is `cmd.exe` reading a .bat file,
# which is why those are pinned to CRLF below.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF or they will not run — including the husky hooks.
*.sh        text eol=lf
.husky/*    text eol=lf

# Windows batch files must be CRLF for cmd.exe to parse them.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch, never diff.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary

# Generated. Keeps `git diff` and PR reviews readable.
package-lock.json   linguist-generated=true -diff
